course: dictionary
lesson: 234
title: Alliteration: the same sound, close together
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Alliteration is when several words close together start with the same sound. «Peter Piper picked a peck of pickled peppers» repeats the p sound again and again, so it is a tongue-twister.
explain: Listen for the first sound, not just the first letter. Words that start with the same letter can still break the pattern, so say them out loud. «Great grey-green, greasy» has the same gr sound at the start of each word, so it is alliteration.
explain: A word that sounds like what it means, such as «buzz» or «hiss», is onomatopoeia, not alliteration. «Buzz» on its own repeats no sound, so it does not count.
explain: Writers use alliteration to make phrases fun to say, easy to remember or dramatic. Tongue-twisters, brand names like PayPal and cartoon names like Peppa Pig all use it.
fact: Alliteration means the same first sound repeated in words that are close together.
fact: «Alliteration» is the technique itself. «An alliteration» is one example of it in a poem, story, title or slogan.
fact: «Slithering snakes slide silently» repeats the s sound, which even sounds a little like a snake.
fact: Onomatopoeia is different: a word that sounds like its meaning, such as «buzz», «hiss» or «pop».
fact: In reading tests you may be asked to find an alliteration, name it and say what effect it has.
exercise: Which phrase uses alliteration? | Tall green trees | Buzz went the bee | The sun was hot | *Peter Piper picked pickled peppers
exercise: Which of these is an alliteration? | hiss | pop | buzz | *Mickey Mouse
exercise: In Tennyson's «He clasps the crag with crooked hands», which sound is repeated at the start of several words? | the s sound | the m sound | *the hard c sound | the b sound
test: Which is onomatopoeia rather than alliteration? | Peter Piper | *buzz | Mickey Mouse | Peppa Pig
test: What is the main effect of alliteration? | It stops words from rhyming | *It makes a phrase easy to remember and fun to say | Shortens a sentence | It makes every word a question
exam: In Tennyson's «He clasps the crag with crooked hands», which technique gives an eagle hands? | onomatopoeia | *personification | simile | alliteration
